<!--
beet's own entry: the dev commands, wired as routes over the capabilities linked
into the `beet` binary.

The wasm test runner depends on it: `.cargo/config.toml` sets
`runner = 'beet run-wasm'`, so `cargo test --target wasm32-...` invokes
`beet run-wasm <module>`, which discovers this entry and dispatches the
`<RunWasm/>` route. The rest are convenience: build, check, static export, pdf,
s3 sync.

It is a one-shot `<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>` root with the `Router`
as its child: the binary declares only that this load should run itself, the
root's `CallOnReady` calls it with the process request, it hops down to the
router, the positional route arg selects the command, and the command resolves
and the process exits. `always=true` makes this dispatcher run on every boot
regardless of `--server`, since `--server` selects a *declared server's*
transports, not this dispatcher's. A `CliServer` is strict by requirement: a
flag the command does not take is refused, so `beet build-wasm --relase`
answers `did you mean --release?` rather than building a debug module.

    cargo install --path crates/beet-cli --all-features   # then, from anywhere in the repo:
    beet run-wasm <module.wasm>            # run a wasm test module
    beet check examples/beetmania           # lint a no-code site
    beet compile examples/beetmania         # compile it into ./.beet/repo
    beet export-static examples/beetmania   # write its dist/
    just build-wasm-min                    # the smallest browser runtime (target/wasm/beet-min.wasm)
    just build-wasm-full                   # ..and the kitchen sink (target/wasm/beet-full.wasm)

Serving a site is NOT a command here. A site entry is its own CLI, so it is
launched directly and names its own verb, exactly as its deploy verbs are
(`--entry` takes a directory holding an entry document, or the entry file itself):

    beet --entry=site serve --server=http       # the beet website (site/main.bsx)
    beet --entry=site serve --server=tui        # ..as a live terminal
    beet --entry=site serve --server=http,ssh   # ..http plus a multi-tenant ssh terminal
    beet --entry=examples/beetmania serve --server=http   # beetmania, a zero-code BSX site

That is the identical path the deployed box takes (`app --home=s3://.. serve`),
and it is why the site's resources and deploy verbs live in `site/main.bsx`: the
application that runs on them is the thing that declares them (`just beet-deploy`
runs `beet --entry=site all/deploy`, the site's package deploy).

What lives here is this WORKSPACE's own shared asset bucket
(`beet--shared--assets`), the source of record for the files too large for git
that the examples and tests read out of `./assets`. Build outputs (the wasm
binaries) are never in it: they live under `target/` and are built by whatever
uses them. It is
public-read so a fresh checkout hydrates without credentials, and it is declared
under a `shared`-stage `<Stack>` because a source shared by developers is not
owned by any deploy stage. A resource belongs to the stack it is authored under
and to no other, so no stage deploy touches it.

    just beet-shared apply                 # provision the workspace assets bucket
    just beet-shared pull                  # hydrate ./assets from it (no credentials)
    just beet-shared push                  # publish ./assets to it

A lean `beet` (the default build, ie `beet run-wasm`) does not load that subtree
at all: `bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra"` excludes it before the build
walk, so none of its tags resolve and none of its deploy machinery is touched.
What remains in its place is a tombstone that keeps the `shared` route
addressable, so `--help` still lists it and dispatching a shared verb reports
the condition that excluded it rather than 404ing.

The shared verbs need the AWS deploy pair, plus `tofu`. That pair is this
entry's own IAM user, `beet-deploy` (named for its `app_name`), holding the
managed policy of the `beet` app and nothing else, kept in line with the
declarations by an elevated deploy (`shared/deploy --elevated`; see
`crates/beet_infra/README.md`, The AWS deploy credential). Every secret this
entry and the workspace's tests read (the model-provider keys, this entry's AWS
pair, the live test accounts' app passwords) is a record of the repo's
`secrets.toml`,
age-sealed to the humans in its `default` group and loaded into the process
environment by the launch before this entry builds (`<Secrets/>` below): there
is no `.env`. A secrets document holding a deploy credential belongs to exactly
one entry, so `site/` and `examples/beetmania` keep their own, each beside its
`main.bsx`. The provider addresses (a region, a Cloudflare account or zone)
are identifiers, not secrets: spreads on a stack or an ancestor, resolved by
ancestry and never read from the environment. Reading the document needs
this machine's age identity (`~/.config/beet/age/keys.txt`); `beet
secrets/check` says whether it does, `beet secrets/ls` what is there, and each
record's note and roll say what it is and where it is re-minted.

The `vault` verbs (`beet vault/keygen`, `backup`, `encrypt`, `decrypt`, ..)
manage the identity and any age file named by path or store uri; the
`secrets` verbs (`beet secrets/ls`, `set`, `get`, `check`, ..) the document.
Both ride the `vault` feature (on by default natively, off in the lean browser
builds), so the routes are excluded where the verbs are not; the declaration
stays, an inert tag in a lean build.
-->
<CliServer always=true {CallOnReady}>
<Router {HelpHandler}>
	<PackageConfig title="Beet" app_name="beet" description="An Atmospheric OS for homegrown tech" version="0.0.9-dev.16"/>
	<RunWasm/>
	<BuildWasm/>
	<Check/>
	<Compile/>
	<AnalyticsReport/>
	<ExportStatic/>
	<ExportPdf/>
	<CaptureScreenshot/>
	<SyncStores/>
	<!-- the workspace's credentials, loaded into the process environment by
	     the launch before this entry builds; unconditional so the prescan
	     sees it (a lean build keeps it as an inert tag) -->
	<Secrets/>
	<Route path="vault" bx:cfg="feature:vault"><VaultRoutes/></Route>
	<Route path="secrets" bx:cfg="feature:vault"><SecretsRoutes/></Route>
	<!-- this machine's OWN aws identity, top level because the safe path has
	     to be the shortest path: `beet aws -- sts get-caller-identity` runs as
	     the read-only `beet-agent`, or under a live admin session, and says
	     which on stderr every time. `beet admin` buys that session with one
	     code from a phone, the one thing an automated process cannot supply -->
	<AwsExec bx:cfg="feature:vault && feature:aws"/>
	<AdminElevate bx:cfg="feature:vault && feature:aws"/>
	<!-- the aws identity this entry deploys as: one iam user, `beet-deploy`,
	     sealed in this entry's document, and one managed policy per app the
	     entry declares, here the `beet` app of the stack below. A deploy checks
	     it against the declarations before it writes anything, and an elevated
	     deploy converges it as an administrator: the scoped pair it seals
	     cannot mint itself -->
	<AwsDeployCredential bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra"/>
	<!-- the repo's own AWS account, a member of the Organization the AWS deploy
	     credential lives and deploys in. The root carries no region, so it is
	     spread here: the state bucket and the Recycle Bin rules land there.
	     Identity Center names its own: the instance's primary region, fixed
	     when it was enabled and independent of where the stacks deploy -->
	<AwsAccount name="beet" bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra" {(AwsRegion("us-west-2"), IdentityCenter{group:"beet-operators", region:"ap-southeast-2"})}/>
	<!-- a person's Identity Center sign in, the human path into every member
	     account: `beet sso login`, then `beet aws --account=beet -- ..` -->
	<Route path="sso" bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra"><SsoRoutes/></Route>
	<!-- `close-account --elevated`: the package's most final act, stated where
	     the account is -->
	<CloseDeployAccounts bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra"/>
	<Route path="shared" bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra">
		<!-- the region is an address declared on the stack, never read from
		     the environment: a region that could fall back would plan a full
		     replacement -->
		<Stack stage="shared" {AwsRegion("us-west-2")}>
			<S3BucketBlock bx:ref="assets" label="assets" deploy_versioned=false public_read=true force_destroy=false object_versioning=true/>
			<!-- the shared assets stack has one resource and no step outside
			     terraform, so its deploy is an apply and its teardown is that
			     read backwards: `tofu destroy`, then the state carriers a deploy
			     needed before it. -->
			<Group bx:ref="deploy_up">
				<TofuApply/>
			</Group>
			<Group bx:ref="deploy_down">
				<StackTeardown/>
				<TofuDestroy/>
			</Group>
			<DeployRoutes deploy={$deploy_up} destroy={$deploy_down}/>
			<Route path="push" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<StoreSync src="fs:assets" dest={$assets}/>
			</Route>
			<Route path="pull" {ExchangeSequence}>
				<StoreSync src={$assets} dest="fs:assets" anonymous=true/>
			</Route>
		</Stack>
	</Route>
	<!-- the stores the live store suites run against, as this entry's own
	     deploy credential (`cargo test -p beet_net --features
	     aws,ureq,rustls-tls --lib -- --ignored`): provisioned once rather
	     than made by each test, so the credential never needs to create or
	     remove a store, only to hold the service. -->
	<Route path="test" bx:cfg="feature:infra && feature:extra">
		<Stack stage="test" {AwsRegion("us-west-2")}>
			<DynamoTableBlock label="dynamo"/>
			<S3BucketBlock label="store" deploy_versioned=false runtime_write=true accept_data_loss=true/>
			<S3BucketBlock label="table" deploy_versioned=false runtime_write=true accept_data_loss=true/>
			<Group bx:ref="test_up">
				<TofuApply/>
			</Group>
			<Group bx:ref="test_down">
				<StackTeardown/>
				<TofuDestroy/>
			</Group>
			<DeployRoutes deploy={$test_up} destroy={$test_down}/>
		</Stack>
	</Route>
</Router>
</CliServer>
